  <title>New publications from the ONDA Lab</title>
  <dc:creator>News </dc:creator>
  <link>https://sancamilloirccs-ondalab.github.io/posts/news-title/new-papers-2026-09.html</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 




<section id="summary" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="summary">Summary</h2>
<section id="brain-state-shapes-paired-associative-stimulation" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="brain-state-shapes-paired-associative-stimulation">Brain state shapes paired associative stimulation</h3>
<p>In this collaboration with Alessio Avenanti (Unibo) group we studied how Premotor-motor paired associative stimulation (ccPAS) induced selective, lasting plasticity only when delivered during movement execution: when you stimulate matters as much as where.</p>
<p>Turrini S, Amadori A, Zago S, Arcara G, Avenanti A (2026). Timing and circuit engagement shape state-dependent plasticity in the ventral premotor-motor pathway. <em>Clinical Neurophysiology</em>, 191, 2112352. DOI: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.clinph.2026.2112352">10.1016/j.clinph.2026.2112352</a></p>
</section>
<section id="pragmatic-deficits-after-stroke-are-not-just-a-right-hemisphere-matter-preprint" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="pragmatic-deficits-after-stroke-are-not-just-a-right-hemisphere-matter-preprint">Pragmatic deficits after stroke are not just a right-hemisphere matter (preprint)</h3>
<p>In 99 stroke patients, left- and right-hemisphere lesions led to largely comparable pragmatic impairments, challenging the view of pragmatics as a right-hemisphere function. This work is part of a long-term collaboration with the group of [Valentina Bambini] and <a href="https://www.neplab.it/">NEP Lab</a> (IUSS Pavia), wonderfully led by <a href="https://sites.google.com/view/simone-gastaldon-phd/?pli=1&amp;authuser=0">S. Gastaldon</a>.</p>
<p>Gastaldon S, Romeo F, Barattieri di San Pietro C, Chumakova N, D’Imperio D, Lago S, Nordio S, Parrotta I, Rigoni M, Bambini V, Arcara G (2026). Challenging the right-hemisphere assumption in post-stroke pragmatics: largely comparable impairment profiles across lesion sides. <em>bioRxiv</em>. DOI: <a href="https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.08.06.742887">10.64898/2026.08.06.742887</a></p>
